## Context

Author request, 2026-11-10: "Normalize encoding and line endings in the
project. Linux".

Survey of the 167 tracked files at d9f86d9:

- Encoding is already uniform: every text file decodes as UTF-8 (or its ASCII
  subset), with no byte-order mark, no non-NFC text, no stray control or
  invisible characters, and a final newline everywhere.
- Line endings are not: 13 files are stored with CRLF in the index
  (`git ls-files --eol` prints `i/crlf`): eight modules under `src/doifetch/`,
  three tests, `LICENSE` and `.claude/plans/atomic-hugging-origami.md`. They
  are leftovers of the Windows-authored upstream; none mixes endings.
- Nothing prevents a recurrence: the repository has no `.gitattributes`, and
  `ruff format` keeps whatever ending a file already has.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `git ls-files --eol` reports no `i/crlf` or `i/mixed` entry
- [ ] A `.gitattributes` makes git store text as LF and check it out as LF on every platform; `tickets/erg` is declared binary
- [ ] An `.editorconfig` declares UTF-8 and LF for editors
- [ ] The conversion changes no behaviour: each converted Python file parses to the same AST as before, and each converted file differs from its old blob only by carriage returns
- [ ] The project's own gate passes
